section.guide
  article
    markdown:
      On GitHub Actions, you can [run Danger without a personal access token](#github-actions-without-a-personal-access-token), including on pull requests from forks.

      For other CI setups, there are 5 steps involved in getting Danger running:

      * [Include Danger](#including-danger).
      * [Creating a Dangerfile](#creating-a-dangerfile) and adding a few simple rules.
      * [Creating an account for Danger to use](#creating-a-bot-account-for-danger-to-use).
      * Setting up [an access token for Danger](#setting-up-an-access-token) with that account.
      * Setting up [Danger to run on your CI](#setting-up-danger-to-run-on-your-ci).

      ### Including Danger

      We recommend you install Danger via [Bundler][bundler] + a Gemfile. This means that your version of Danger and your plugins are all versioned correctly. You are in control of how and when your dependencies are updated. If you'd like to learn more about Bundler, check out [this guide][cp_bundler].

      ##### Installation

      If you have an existing Gemfile, add `gem 'danger'` or `gem 'danger-gitlab'` to it.  If you don't, run `bundle init` in your project root, and edit the freshly-minted Gemfile. 

      ##### Bundler 101

      Bundler is a dependency manager for Ruby which uses a Gemfile to define all of the Ruby projects you want to use. To get started type in `bundler init` in your project folder.
      This creates your `Gemfile`. Open this up in your editor, then replace the ` #gem 'rails'` with the gem above. Then run `bundle install` inside your project folder.

      ### Easy Mode

      *Easy mode* - run: `bundle exec danger init`. The wizard detects existing CI configuration and asks you to choose when multiple providers are present. When none is detected, it offers GitHub Actions first for repositories with a GitHub remote. The GitHub Actions setup creates a Dangerfile and two workflows, preserves existing files, and explains the required `danger-pr-comment` dependency. Other CI setups retain the bot-account and token walkthrough. If you would like to understand how all of the pieces come together, read on:

      ### Creating a Dangerfile

      Create an empty file named `Dangerfile`. The file is written in Ruby, but your text editor might not recognize it as such, so you may need to set the syntax highlighting manually (unless you're [using VS Code][vs_code]). To get started, we would recommend a simple "Hello World."

      ```
      message("Hello, this worked")
      ```

      ### GitHub Actions without a personal access token

      GitHub Actions provides an automatic, short-lived `GITHUB_TOKEN`; you do not need a bot account, personal access token, or repository secret. For pull requests from forks, that token normally cannot post comments. Use two workflows: run Danger with read-only permissions on `pull_request`, then post its JSON report in a separate `workflow_run` workflow with comment permissions.

      The community-maintained [danger-pr-comment](https://github.com/numbata/danger-pr-comment) gem and reusable workflows implement this setup. Add these gems to your Gemfile and run `bundle install`:

      ```ruby
      gem 'danger'
      gem 'danger-pr-comment', require: false
      ```

      Your project can use any language. If there is no Gemfile, `danger init` creates a tooling Gemfile with the RubyGems source and these dependencies. Existing Gemfiles are preserved; add any missing dependencies yourself. Run `bundle install` and commit the Gemfile and Gemfile.lock.

      Generated workflows use `.ruby-version` or the Ruby entry in `.tool-versions` when available. Otherwise, the wizard sets `ruby-version: '3.4'` for Danger's tooling, including in non-Ruby projects. Adjust it to match your dependencies if needed.

      Import the report exporter before your Dangerfile rules:

      ```ruby
      danger.import_dangerfile(gem: 'danger-pr-comment')

      message("Hello, this worked")
      ```

      Create `.github/workflows/danger.yml`:

      ```yaml
      name: Danger
      on:
        pull_request:
          types: [opened, reopened, edited, synchronize]

      permissions:
        contents: read
        pull-requests: read

      jobs:
        danger:
          uses: numbata/danger-pr-comment/.github/workflows/danger-run.yml@58e325dd25ef3e26cdcd60c97e6ec7848a7d7fdf
          with:
            ruby-version: '3.4'
            bundler-cache: true
            danger-args: 'pr ${{ github.event.pull_request.html_url }} --fail-on-errors=true'
      ```

      Create `.github/workflows/danger-comment.yml`:

      ```yaml
      name: Danger Comment
      on:
        workflow_run:
          workflows: [Danger]
          types: [completed]

      permissions:
        actions: read
        issues: write
        pull-requests: write

      jobs:
        comment:
          if: github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'success' || github.event.workflow_run.conclusion == 'failure'
          uses: numbata/danger-pr-comment/.github/workflows/danger-comment.yml@58e325dd25ef3e26cdcd60c97e6ec7848a7d7fdf
      ```

      Choose a Ruby version compatible with your project. Both examples pin the reusable workflows to a commit; review updates before changing that revision. No `secrets: inherit` is required.

      The first workflow runs `danger pr` against the triggering pull request without posting results. This provides the normal `github` plugin and PR metadata, unlike the local-only `danger dry_run` command. `--fail-on-errors=true` fails the job when rules report errors, while the report is still uploaded. The second workflow downloads the report and creates or updates the PR comment using GitHub's automatic token. Both workflow files must be present on the default branch for the `workflow_run` integration to work.

      Keep the commenting workflow limited to processing report data: do not check out or execute pull request code with write permissions. Do not switch to `pull_request_target` to run untrusted Dangerfiles with a privileged token.

      Danger's own [CI workflow](https://github.com/danger/danger/blob/master/.github/workflows/CI.yml) uses this approach alongside its existing test matrix. Its [commenting workflow](https://github.com/danger/danger/blob/master/.github/workflows/CI-comment.yml) verifies that the report identifies a pull request whose head commit, branch, and repository match the triggering run before allowing the comment job to proceed.

      See [dblock/ruby-enum](https://github.com/dblock/ruby-enum), including its [Dangerfile](https://github.com/dblock/ruby-enum/blob/master/Dangerfile) and [workflows](https://github.com/dblock/ruby-enum/tree/master/.github/workflows), for a working example. With this setup, skip the bot-account and access-token steps below.

      ### Creating a bot account for Danger to use

      This is optional. Pragmatically, you want to do this though.

